  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About Georgian writing",
      "body": "<p>Georgian has had three alphabets: Asomtavruli (from about the 5th century), Nuskhuri (from the 9th) and Mkhedruli (from the 11th). Mkhedruli, the rounded one taught here, is the one used for everyday Georgian. All three are on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage.</p>\n<p>The script is also used for Georgia's other Kartvelian languages: Mingrelian, Svan and Laz.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "No capital letters",
      "body": "<p>Mkhedruli has one case. Sentences and names start with the same letters as any other word.</p>\n<p>There is a set of capital-like forms called Mtavruli, used in titles, signs and headlines written all in capitals: <span lang=\"ka\">ᲡᲐᲥᲐᲠᲗᲕᲔᲚᲝ</span> for <span lang=\"ka\">საქართველო</span> (Sakartvelo, Georgia). They are not used to start sentences.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "One letter, one sound",
      "body": "<p>Georgian spelling is very regular: each letter has one sound, and words are said as they are written. There are no silent letters and no stress marks to learn.</p>\n<p>The challenge is in the sounds themselves. Georgian contrasts three versions of many consonants (voiced, with a puff of air, and ejective), and it allows long clusters of consonants at the start of a word: <span lang=\"ka\">გვფრცქვნი</span> (gvprtskvni, you peel us) has eight in a row.</p>"
    }
  ]
